what the numbers say
  - best hit rate is not unique to one variant.
  - sma18 + atr14 + mult1.2 + win3 is top or near-top on both days and both directions.
  - sma20 + atr14 + mult1.0 + win3 has higher avg_rr in several cuts, but slightly lower hit rate.
  - sma16 and atr10 rows have lower hit rate and worse stop rate in this data set.
  - small changes around the best row stay close in hit rate, so the result looks stable.
  - top3 day concentration is around 0.30-0.32, so the result is not one amazing day only.

ranking conclusion
  primary candidate   : sma18_a14_m12_w3
    why   : highest combined hit rate, good sample size, stable across both days,
            usable on both LONG and SHORT.
    caution: avg_rr is slightly lower than sma20_a14_m10_w3 in some cuts.

  secondary candidate : sma20_a14_m10_w3
    why   : higher avg_rr in several views, still strong hit rate and sample size.
    caution: slightly lower hit rate and slightly more no-entry behavior.

  rejected as final pick
    sma16 rows    : lower hit rate, worse stop rate.
    atr10 rows    : weaker overall and more stop-heavy in this data.
    wide variants : not enough advantage outside the top band.

recommended next step
  1. freeze sma18_a14_m12_w3 as the first chosen candidate.
  2. run the next mode on top of it, not beside it.
  3. keep sma20_a14_m10_w3 as a comparison candidate if avg_rr matters more.
  4. confirm the chosen candidate still holds on new days/timeframes before final.

one-line conclusion
  Best usable candidate from this cartesian run:
  sma=18, atr=14, mult=1.2, window=3.
  It gives the best balance of hit rate, sample size, stability, and downside.
  sma=20, atr=14, mult=1.0, window=3 is a fair secondary option,
  but it is not clearly better overall.
